<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mamikonian dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mamikonian family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mamikonian family was one of the most powerful noble houses of ancient and early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, holding the hereditary office of commander-in-chief (sparapet) under the Arsacid kings. Its members led the Armenian defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> against Sasanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, most famously <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardan-mamikonian">Vardan Mamikonian</a>, killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-avarayr">Battle of Avarayr</a> in 451. The family declined after failed revolts against Arab rule in the 8th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origin of the Mamikonians is unknown. Movses Khorenatsi tells that a nobleman named Mamgon fled from the land of Chenk, thought to mean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, to the Persian court and was sent on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, where King Trdat gave him land; the Primary History attributed to Sebeos names two brothers, Mamik and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qudshanis">Konak</a>. Pavstos Buzand also gives them royal Chen descent, equal to that of the Arsacids. Nicholas Adontz, Cyril Toumanoff and Robert Hewsen instead see them as old dynasts of Tayk, connecting Chenk with the Georgian Tzans, while others place it near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Besides the office of sparapet, the family held that of dayeak, royal tutor, which let it act as kingmaker, and it ruled Taron, Tayk, Sasun, Bagrevand and other lands. The first lord known with certainty is Vache Mamikonian, active in 330–339. In the 4th century Vasak Mamikonian led the defence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> until he was lured to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and flayed alive, and his son Mushegh I restored King Pap with help from the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>. Hamazasp Mamikonian's marriage to a daughter of Patriarch Isaac the Great brought much church land to the family.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardan-mamikonian">Vardan Mamikonian</a>'s death at Avarayr, his nephew Vahan Mamikonian kept up the revolt until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nvarsak">Treaty of Nvarsak</a> of 484 restored Armenian autonomy and religious rights, and he became marzpan, Persian governor, in 485. The family often sided with the Eastern Roman Empire, and the failed revolt of Vardan II Mamikonian in the late 6th century began its decline. Under Arab rule the rival Bagratunis gained favour, took the office of sparapet in the 8th century and by 750 held Taron. Grigor Mamikonian's rising ended with his flight to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> around 748.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The final blow came in the mid-770s, when Mushegh Mamikonian fell at the Battle of Bagrevand against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. His sons were murdered, and his daughter's marriage to the tribal chief Djahap al-Qais founded the Kaysite emirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> on former Mamikonian lands. Lesser lines lasted until the later 9th century. Some Georgian houses and Byzantine families, among them the emperor Philippikos Bardanes, have been claimed as descendants, though Nina Garsoïan judges this cannot be proven. Several princes were buried at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surb-karapet-monastery">Surb Karapet Monastery</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mus">Mush</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mamikonian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Bagrevand, the caliphal army crushed Armenian princes who had rebelled against the Abbasid Caliphate, killing their main leaders and almost extinguishing the power of the <b>Mamikonian family</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-25">25 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings ruled from Artaxata and later Vagharshapat over powerful hereditary noble houses such as the <b>Mamikonians</b> and Bagratunis.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</a></li></ul></section>
